    <h2>Why differential</h2>
    <p>&ldquo;This package gained network and process-spawn capabilities
    between 1.2.3 and 1.2.4, and the changelog says <em>fix typo</em>&rdquo;
    is a far higher-signal alert than any absolute score on a whole tree.
    Capability drift mismatched against stated intent is exactly the shape of
    xz-utils, event-stream, and ua-parser-js. Judging only the delta also
    keeps dependency bumps fast to check in CI, and keeps the
    false-positive budget tiny.</p>

    <h2>What isomer judges</h2>
    <ul class="feature-list">
      <li><strong>Capability diff</strong> — trait extraction on both sides; the set of gained and lost capabilities is the core feature.</li>
      <li><strong>Version-drift proportionality</strong> — a patch release shouldn't produce a major-release-sized behavioral shift.</li>
      <li><strong>Class-vs-capability mismatch</strong> — JPEG libraries shouldn't be talking to the internet.</li>
      <li><strong>Commit intent</strong> — commits that introduce security degradations their messages don't mention (LLM-backed when available, heuristic otherwise).</li>
      <li><strong>Behavioral disassembly</strong> — rizin-derived behavior, so the diff works on stripped, compiled artifacts too.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Surface</h2>
<pre class="install-block">isomer ci                             # zero-config in CI: derives base..head from the environment
isomer fs   &lt;old-path&gt; &lt;new-path&gt;     # compare two trees; follows the dependency graph
isomer git  --repo &lt;url&gt; &lt;old&gt; &lt;new&gt;  # compare two commits/branches/tags of a remote repo
isomer purl &lt;purl@a&gt; &lt;purl@b&gt;         # compare two published package versions
isomer oci  &lt;old-image&gt; &lt;new-image&gt;   # compare two container images</pre>
    <p>Argument order is always old, then new, like <code>diff</code>;
    <code>--base</code>/<code>--head</code> flags are accepted everywhere for
    order-proof CI invocations. Exit code 0 means clean, 1 means findings at
    or above <code>--fail-on</code>, and 2 means an operational error —
    never conflated with findings.</p>

    <h2>The xz-utils test</h2>
    <p>isomer's benchmark is the hardest version of the problem: the
    compiled, stripped <code>liblzma.so</code> from the 2024 xz-utils
    backdoor. The 5.4.5&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;5.6.0 diff verdicts
    <strong>HOSTILE</strong> — and with every known signature ignored, the
    behavioral axis alone (<code>ifunc-resolver-hijack</code>, hidden byte
    strings, XOR encoding) still reads high enough to fail CI, which is what
    catching a <em>novel</em> xz-shaped attack on release day requires. The
    5.6.0&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;5.6.3 diff correctly reads as the backdoor being
    <em>removed</em>, not added.</p>

